Video pictures kept on the GPU: Android's hardware decoders (0015) draw into an AImageReader's
Android hardware buffers (AIMAGE_FORMAT_PRIVATE, the GPU's own YUV layout), each picture made a
shared image (gpu::SpatiandHardwareBufferImageBacking, GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES bound to an EGLImage
of the buffer, the decoder's fence waited on first) that the compositor samples as it is, and given
back to the decoder when its video frame is let go -- as the Deck's VA-API pictures stay on its
GPU. Pictures are drawn into the reader one at a time (one drawn while the one before is still in
the reader's queue replaced it: 60-frame H.264 reached the page at 42 frames a second), and the
reader holds 24, the renderer's own cap (with ten 60 frames a second played at 36). Where that
cannot be set up (no GPU channel, a reader or a codec that will not take one) the pictures are
copied out into frames of memory as before. ANGLE (third_party/angle, a repository of its own; its
part after Chromium's, as 0021 carries it) samples such a buffer through a Y'CbCr conversion, with
the external format Vulkan knows it by (AHardwareBufferImageSiblingVkSpatiand, vk_cache_utils), as
its Android build does. The GPU sandbox's part (Android's ashmem, without which no decoder started)
is in 0016. Measured on the Beam Pro (2026-11-17), 1080p60 HEVC: 45 frames a second with 150
dropped before, 59 with 2 to 9 dropped after.

